{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-core":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-core/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-first-order-taylor","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:first-order-taylor","kind":"remark","title":"First-order approximation","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-su2-basis"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-su2-basis","tags":["lie-algebra","baker-campbell-hausdorff"],"text":"first-order-taylor.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:first-order-taylor\"></a><p>The Lie bracket captures the first-order behaviour of the group operation. For elements <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">A,B</code> near the identity:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  e^A e^B =  e^{A+B+\\tfrac{1}{2}[A,B]+\\cdots}.</code></pre>\n<p>The commutator <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[e_a,e_b]</code> acts as the first-order Taylor polynomial of the non-commutativity. Higher-order corrections forming the full Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff series encode the complete nonlinear group law.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/braids-and-knots/first-order-taylor.md","heading":"Remark","number":"7.5.2","page":"b/rem-first-order-taylor","qa":{"counts":{"pass":62,"fail":0,"warn":0,"na":15,"unknown":0,"stale":0},"milnor":{"total":2,"notPassing":0}}}
